  "result": "I like to imagine that lost things gather somewhere after we stop looking for them. Not in a grand warehouse, but in a quiet seaside town where the streets are narrow and every window catches the afternoon light. Single gloves hang from balconies. Keys bloom in bowls beside unfamiliar doors.\n\nThere is a caf\u00e9 for forgotten names. They sit at little tables, stirring their tea, waiting for someone far away to pause halfway through a sentence. Whenever a name is remembered, its chair becomes empty. The waiter clears the cup without surprise and opens a window toward the waking world.\n\nThe largest building belongs to misplaced afternoons: hours spent worrying about troubles that never arrived, or scrolling past things already forgotten. Inside, those hours have become rooms. Some contain rain. Some contain music. Some hold nothing but a comfortable chair and the strange relief of having nowhere else to be.\n\nI think we would recognize the place immediately, though none of us could explain how to get there. We would find our childhood courage repairing bicycles near the harbor, our abandoned hobbies selling oranges in the square, our old ambitions happily doing work we had never thought to give them.\n\nPerhaps nothing there wants to be found. Perhaps that is the kindness of it. The missing scarf has learned the weather. The unwritten letter has made friends. And when evening comes, all our lost things walk down to the water, unburdened by our needing them, and watch the tide return.",
